Raft current at high frame rates (Level B1, disc Lvl-05)

On PC the raft that the river should carry through the gate in Level B1 can take a very long time to start moving. The raft
takes the current's speed toward the water zone's (100, 200 or 400 units/s) with Math_ApproachLinear, whose step per
frame is 50 * g_dt >> 12 (g_dt is the frame's length in 1/4096 s). The shift drops the fraction: at any frame rate
above about 50 FPS the step is 0, so the current only builds up on lag frames.

This fix moves the current's speed by the step of a 30 FPS frame once for every 1/30 s of game time, however many frames
that time is split into. At any frame rate the raft now picks up the current as it does at 30 FPS: after 3 seconds on a
100 units/s current it is at 90 units/s at 30, 60 and 144 FPS alike (the original: 90, 0 and 0).

Not changed: the raft's position still moves by velocity * g_dt / 4096 per frame, truncated per axis, as every object
in the game does. At 60 FPS that makes a slow current carry the raft somewhat slower than at 30 FPS.

Touches: src/objects/raft.cpp (Raft::Update, and a new static Raft_ApproachFlowSpeed).

diff --git a/src/objects/raft.cpp b/src/objects/raft.cpp
index 3a54cbe..ca70999 100644
--- a/src/objects/raft.cpp
+++ b/src/objects/raft.cpp
@@ -132,6 +132,37 @@ void Raft::Render(Camera *view)
         wakeEmitter.RenderFlat(view, 1);
 }
 
+extern u32 g_gameTime; /* 0x71b2d0  game time in 1/4096 s: advances by g_dt every frame */
+extern s32 g_dt;       /* 0x71b300  this frame's length in 1/4096 s */
+
+/* FIX raft-current-high-fps: the original takes the current's speed toward the zone's with
+ * Math_ApproachLinear(cur, target, 2000, 50, 100), whose step is 50 * g_dt >> 12 (100 when slowing down). The shift
+ * drops the fraction, so on a frame shorter than 82/4096 s (any frame rate above about 50 FPS) the step is 0 and the
+ * current never builds up: the raft only gains speed on lag frames. Here the speed moves by the step of a 30 FPS frame
+ * (136/4096 s: +1 speeding up, -3 slowing down) once for every 1/30 s of game time, however many frames that time is
+ * split into, so the raft picks up the current as it does at 30 FPS at any frame rate. The limit and the clamping are
+ * Math_ApproachLinear's. */
+static s32 Raft_ApproachFlowSpeed(s32 cur, s32 target)
+{
+    s32 periods = (s32)(g_gameTime / 136 - (g_gameTime - g_dt) / 136); /* whole 1/30 s ended this frame */
+    s32 step = periods * ((((target >= cur) ? 50 : 100) * 136) >> 12);
+    if ((target - cur >= 0 ? target - cur : cur - target) <= step) {
+        if (target > 2000)
+            target = 2000;
+        else if (target < -2000)
+            target = -2000;
+    } else if (target > cur) {
+        target = cur + step;
+        if (target > 2000)
+            target = 2000;
+    } else {
+        target = cur - step;
+        if (target < -2000)
+            target = -2000;
+    }
+    return target;
+}
+
 /* 0x4df4e7 */
 /* BYTES(slot-name, inferred): local names chosen for their stack slots (tools/vc6_locals.py), not recovered */
 void Raft::Update()
@@ -216,7 +247,7 @@ void Raft::Update()
         } else {
             wantedFlowLocal = 0;
         }
-        flowSpeed = Math_ApproachLinear(flowSpeed, wantedFlowLocal, 2000, 50, 100);
+        flowSpeed = Raft_ApproachFlowSpeed(flowSpeed, wantedFlowLocal); /* FIX raft-current-high-fps */
         if (flowSpeed) {
             velocity.x += (s16)((-flowSpeed * g_sinTable4096[flowHeading]) >> 12);
             velocity.z += (s16)((-flowSpeed * g_pCosTable[flowHeading]) >> 12);
